Thank you,
[Your name] | [phone] | [city, state]

C — Typical documents & steps (U.S. SSA)
- What to request:
  - Replacement Social Security card (if you need the card itself), or
  - Proof/verification letter (a record or letter confirming the SSN).
- Documents usually required (originals or certified copies, not photocopies):
  - Proof of identity: U.S. passport, state driver’s license, or state ID.
  - Proof of citizenship or lawful immigration status if the SSA requires it (birth certificate or immigration documents).
- How to submit:
  - Online (if eligible) via ssa.gov/myaccount — for replacement cards in many cases.
  - In-person at your local SSA office (recommended if you need certified documents).
  - By mail (use certified or tracked mail if sending originals).
- Processing times:
  - Replacement card: typically 7–14 business days after processing.
  - Verification letters can vary; ask the office for an estimated timeframe.

D — Safety & privacy tips
- Avoid sending your full SSN in an unsecured email or text. If you must provide it, send only the last four digits and follow the office’s secure upload or in-person instructions.
- If asked to provide documents electronically, use the SSA’s official portal (ssa.gov) or a secure upload mechanism — don’t attach originals to standard email unless explicitly directed and secured.
- If mailing originals, use tracked/certified mail and request return of originals if possible.
- Verify contact info: SSA main phone is 1-800-772-1213 and official website is https://www.ssa.gov. Use local office contact information from the official site.

If you’d like, I can:
- Tailor one of the templates with your specific reason and contact details (without including your full SSN), or
- Provide wording for a phone script if you prefer to call the SSA. Which would you prefer?

