Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — but I won’t repeat your full SSN here. I can show how to request a copy from the Social Security Administration (or other issuing authority) using only the last four digits (which you gave as 1234). Below are steps to take and two short templates you can use (one formal letter, one email). I also include security tips.

What to request / which form
- If you need a replacement Social Security card: use the SSA Form SS-5 (Application for a Social Security Card) and provide required identity documents. You can apply online (if eligible) or at a local SSA office.
- If you need an official record or verification (Numident/SSN verification letter): contact SSA customer service or your local office and ask for the specific document you need.
- If you aren’t in the U.S. or your issuing authority is different, substitute the relevant agency and check their required forms.

What to include in the request
- Your full legal name (as on record)
- Date of birth
- Last four digits of your SSN (do not include the full number in unsecured communications)
- Current mailing address and phone number
- Type of document requested (replacement card, SSN verification letter, copy of record)
- Copies of required ID (e.g., valid driver’s license, passport, state ID; and proof of citizenship or lawful status if required)
- Preferred method for receiving the document (mail, in-person pickup) if applicable

Security and process notes
- Don’t send your full SSN over unsecured email. If the agency has a secure upload/portal, use that.
- Bring or include photocopies of identity documents rather than originals unless the agency requires originals.
- Expect processing time (often 10–14 business days for a replacement card once approved; verification letters may take longer).
- The SSA generally does not charge for a replacement Social Security card.

