I can help. A few quick points before the templates:
- Don’t include your full SSN in an unsecured email — most agencies (including the Social Security Administration, SSA) prefer you provide the full number in person, through a secure upload, or on official forms. If you must identify yourself in an email, give only the last four digits (you already provided “1234”) and ask how they want you to send full details securely.  
- Decide what you need: a replacement Social Security card, an official verification letter, or a verification for a third party (employer/lender). The SSA handles replacement cards (Form SS‑5) and certain verification requests; third parties often use Form SSA‑89 (Authorization for the SSA to release SSN verification).  
- Bring the proper ID and original/certified documents when requested (driver’s license or passport, birth certificate or immigration documents, and proof of name change if applicable). Replacement cards are free. Processing times vary—check ssa.gov or your local office for current estimates.

