Below are short, ready-to-use templates and quick guidance you can adapt.

Quick guidance
- Don’t email your full SSN. Provide only the last four digits in an email/letter and offer to give the full number in person, by phone over a verified number, or via the agency’s secure portal.  
- Include identifying details they’ll need: full name (as on file), date of birth, current address, and a phone number or email.  
- Ask what specific documents they require (e.g., government photo ID, birth certificate, proof of citizenship/immigration) and whether you should complete an application form (for the U.S. Social Security Administration that’s Form SS‑5 for a replacement card).  
- Confirm processing time and how the copy or replacement will be delivered (secure mail, in-person pickup, etc.).  
- Use only official agency contact channels (e.g., SSA.gov for U.S. Social Security) and verify phone numbers/URLs to avoid scams.

