General advice (security and process)
- Prefer secure channels: use the issuing authority’s secure portal, in-person visit, or certified mail. Avoid sending your full SSN in plain, unsecured email.
- In your initial request, give identifying details but share only the last four digits of your SSN (or redact the first five digits as XXX-XX-9012) and ask how to submit the full number securely if needed.
- Ask what specific form(s) and identity documents they require (e.g., Form SS-5 in the US for a replacement card; original or certified copies of a passport, state ID, birth certificate).
- Ask about processing time and whether there are any fees.
- If you want a replacement card vs. a verification letter, say so — the required documents and steps can differ.

