Key practical tips
- Don’t send your full SSN in plain (unencrypted) email. If you must provide the number electronically, use the agency’s secure portal or upload tool, or provide it in person.
- Many agencies will accept the last four digits in initial communications and then request full details through a secure channel.
- For Social Security requests, check SSA.gov first — replacement card requests typically use form SS‑5 and require original identity documents (driver’s license, U.S. passport, or other acceptable ID). Some services are available through a mySocialSecurity account.
- Bring original documents (not photocopies) unless the agency explicitly allows certified copies.
- Ask the office what options they offer (online, mail, in-person) and whether certified copies are available and if there are fees.

