Sincerely,  
[Your name]  
[Signature if mailing]

2) Short email to an employer, HR department, or third party who needs to verify the SSN on file
Subject: Request to confirm SSN on file (SSN ending in 1234)

Hi [HR name],

Could you please confirm the SSN you have on file for me? For security, please only confirm using the last four digits (mine end in 1234) and advise whether you can provide an official verification letter or copy of the document you have. If you need any ID or authorization form from me, let me know and I will provide it through your secure channel.

Thanks,  
[Your name]  
[Contact info]

3) Phone script (calling SSA or HR)
“Hello — my name is [Your name]. I need help verifying or obtaining a replacement Social Security card. My SSN ends in 1234. What documents do you require and what is the process? Can you send instructions by mail or direct me to your secure online option?”

Checklist — documents you will likely need
- U.S. passport or state driver’s license (proof of identity)  
- Birth certificate or proof of citizenship (if required)  
- Proof of legal name change (if applicable)  
- Proof of current address (utility bill, lease, etc.)  
- Completed form if required (for SSA, Form SS-5 for a replacement card)

Security and formatting tips
- Don’t include your full SSN in an email or text. Use only “SSN ending in 1234” when asking.  
- Request secure delivery (in-person pickup, mailed letter, or agency secure portal). Avoid sending the full SSN over unencrypted email.  
- Use the official agency website (e.g., ssa.gov in the U.S.) to find instructions and forms. Beware of scams — confirm phone numbers from the official site.  
- If asking an employer/HR, ask for an “official verification letter” rather than a scanned card copy whenever possible.

What to expect (U.S. SSA)
- For a replacement Social Security card, you typically submit Form SS-5 plus ID docs; you may need to apply in person or mail originals (or certified copies). Processing times vary by office.  
- Agencies usually will not send full SSNs via email or unsecured channels. They may verify the last four digits or provide an official letter on agency letterhead.

If you’d like, tell me:
- Who you’re sending this to (SSA, employer HR, state agency, etc.), and
