--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 4177 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can — below are simple, secure ways to phrase a request and guidance on what to include. I won’t repeat your full SSN here; I’ll refer only to the last four digits you gave (1234). If you want, I can tailor any template to the specific issuing authority (for example, the U.S. Social Security Administration vs. a state agency) and to whether you plan to send email, mail, or visit in person.

Key points before you write
- Prefer not to transmit the full SSN over insecure email. Use a secure portal, in-person visit, or certified mail when providing the full number or identity documents.  
- State your identity clearly (full name as on file, date of birth) and give the last four digits of the SSN to help them locate the record. If they require the full SSN, ask how to provide it securely.  
- Say why you need the copy (e.g., replacement card, proof for employer, legal/benefits application) — agencies often treat requests differently depending on purpose.  
- Include contact info and list or attach the identity documents you can provide (driver’s license, passport, birth certificate, etc.). Ask what additional documents they need and about processing time.

Formal letter (mail or in-person intake)
[Your name]  
[Your address]  
[City, State ZIP]  
[Phone] | [Email]  
[Date]

To Whom It May Concern,

I am requesting a copy/official verification of my Social Security record (or “a replacement Social Security card,” if that is what you need) for the purpose of [brief reason — e.g., employment verification, benefits application, driver’s license application]. My name is [Full name as on file], date of birth [MM/DD/YYYY], and the last four digits of my Social Security number are 1234.

Please let me know what documents are required and how I should securely provide my full Social Security number and identity documents. I can provide [list IDs you have — e.g., U.S. passport, state driver’s license, birth certificate] and can appear in person or send certified copies if needed.

Thank you for your assistance. Please contact me at [phone] or [email] with instructions and any applicable forms.

Sincerely,  
[Your full name]

Email template (if the agency accepts email)
Subject: Request for Copy/Verification of Social Security Record — [Your Full Name]

Hello,

I am requesting an official copy or verification of my Social Security record for [reason]. My full name is [Full name], DOB [MM/DD/YYYY], and the last four digits of my SSN are 1234. Please advise how I can provide the full SSN and identity documents securely (secure portal, in-person, fax to a verified number, etc.) and what forms or identification you require.

Thank you,  
[Your name]  
[Phone] | [Address]

Phone script
“Hello — my name is [Name]. I need to request a copy/verification of my Social Security record (or a replacement card). My date of birth is [DOB], and the last four digits of my SSN are 1234. What is the secure way to provide my full SSN and identity documents, and what documents or forms do you require? Also, what is the expected processing time?”

Documents commonly requested
- Government-issued photo ID (driver’s license, state ID, passport)  
- Birth certificate or proof of citizenship/immigration status (if applicable)  
- Proof of current address (utility bill, bank statement)  
- Marriage certificate or court order for name changes (if applicable)

Security and practical tips
- Ask the office whether they accept requests via an online “secure message” or the agency’s official portal (e.g., SSA’s mySocialSecurity). Avoid sending a full SSN in plain email.  
- If mailing, use certified mail and include only copies of documents unless originals are explicitly requested.  
- Keep a record of the request (date, person you spoke with, any case/receipt number).  
- Verify you’re contacting the official agency website or phone number (e.g., ssa.gov for U.S. Social Security). Scams often mimic official agencies.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a version of one of the templates addressed to a particular agency (for example, the U.S. Social Security Administration), or  
- Convert one to a shorter message for an online form or secure portal?

