Security and process tips
- Don’t email your full SSN unless the recipient explicitly requests it and you know the channel is secure. Use the last four in initial requests.  
- Check the issuing authority’s website (for US: SSA.gov) first — many requests can be started online or require specific original documents.  
- If mailing originals, use certified or tracked mail; if they require originals and you can’t visit in person, ask whether certified copies are acceptable.  
- If you must include your full SSN in the letter, consider sending by secure methods (in-person or through a secure upload/portal) and note why the full SSN is necessary.  
- Include a clear return method and a phone number so they can reach you with questions.

If you tell me which authority you’re contacting (e.g., Social Security Administration in the U.S., a state agency, a different country’s authority) and whether you want a physical card or a verification letter, I’ll tailor the text and checklist of likely required documents.

